Your rules for this feature, as the builders will follow them (each one applies to every case like it, not just the example):
  1. Whenever a customer returns an item for a change of mind, or gives no reason at all (a return with no reason counts as change of mind), the refund depends on the days since delivery. Within 14 days of delivery it is the full item amount, with no 5% charge deducted and no waiting period before it is paid. From day 15 through day 30 it is half of the item amount. After day 30 it is nothing. A negative number of days counts as day 0.
  2. Whenever a refund amount is worked out, it is item prices times quantities minus the discount code. Store credit used to pay does not reduce it (for example, 1,000 rupees less a 20% coupon is 800 rupees, even if 100 rupees was paid with store credit).
  3. Whenever a return is approved, the shipping or delivery fee is not refunded, unless the item is damaged or faulty. In that case the shipping fee is refunded as well.
  4. Whenever an order is final-sale and the return is not for a damaged item, there is no refund.
  5. Whenever an item is reported damaged or faulty within 90 days of delivery, the refund is the full purchase amount (item prices times quantities minus the discount code) plus the shipping fee, even if the order is final-sale. Whenever it is reported after 90 days, there is no refund.
  6. Whenever a change-of-mind refund comes to less than 50 rupees, it is paid as 0. Whenever a change-of-mind refund amount is worked out, it is rounded down to whole rupees. The 50 rupee minimum does not apply to a damaged or faulty refund.
  7. Whenever a return is refunded, the money goes back the way it was paid. Card payments go to the card and store-credit payments go back as store credit. For a mixed order, the part paid with store credit goes back as store credit and the rest goes to the card (for example, 100 rupees of an 800 rupee refund as store credit and 700 rupees to the card).
  8. Whenever an order is completed, it earns loyalty points as follows. The item amount after the discount code must be 500 rupees or more, otherwise it earns 0 points (a 90 rupee pen earns 0). If it is 500 or more, it earns 1 point per full 100 rupees of that item amount, rounded down (1,250 rupees earns 12 points). Shipping earns nothing. An order paid fully with store credit earns no points. A final-sale order earns double points.
  9. Whenever an order is refunded or cancelled, there is no rule for loyalty points. Nothing is built for this case, and the loyalty rules stay exactly as the owner described them.
  10. Whenever the daily summary is produced, the order count is the number of every order in the list, whatever its status (paid, cancelled, refunded or awaiting payment), including store-credit orders. On a day with no orders it shows 0.
  11. Whenever the daily summary shows revenue, it is the sum of the totals of card orders only, where a card order's total is items after discount plus shipping. Store-credit orders contribute nothing to revenue. On a day with no orders, revenue shows 0.
  12. Whenever the daily summary shows the average, it is revenue divided by the number of card orders, always rounded down to whole rupees (100.33 shows as 100 and 150.5 shows as 150). If there are no card orders, it is 0.
  13. When an order is paid partly with store credit and partly in cash, does it earn loyalty points? -> A) No. An order paid with store credit, in part or in full, earns no points.
